Round 46 is done and live. The treasure ship — last item on the crudest-first queue — was rebuilt from the record, and every fix landed at the class level, so the 34 m Chinese junk inherited all of it.

**What the survey found (12 spin bearings, before):** five white rectangles for sails on the one rig famous for its fanned profile, a European yacht bow on a hull whose own stage card teaches "bulkheads, then planking," open uncapped shell ends, a door-sized invisible rudder, no aft castle, and no running rigging at all.

**What changed, and why it's the record's call:**
- **The sail plan.** The code had cited Reddish's measured junk proportions for eleven rounds while drawing them as a rectangle. His own numbers — yard ⅔ of the boom, leech 1.75 booms — only close as a fan with the yard near 60°. The spars are laid first and the cloth is cut to them panel by panel from the same endpoints, so spar and canvas cannot disagree. Sheetlet crowfeet and halyards are drawn, since being worked from the deck is the junk rig's whole argument.
- **The hull ends.** Bulkhead-built hulls now end in transom caps lofted from the hull's own end sections; stem and sternpost are gated off. The bluntness itself is declared in the data as the ship's own lines, so no other hull moved.
- **The rudder.** Sized from the Longjiang excavation's 11.07 m rudder post: chord near a tenth of the waterline, foot lowered below the bottom where it serves as the leeway board of a keel-less hull — with a documented exemption in the audit's below-the-keel rule, since she docks with it raised in its trunk.
- **The aft castle**, tiered and latticed, lofted off the hull's own half-breadth — and the rig reads the castle from the same data, so the jigger lifts its foot over the roof and shrinks its fan, which is also the record's look.

**Verification:** audit 25/25 with three new rules (bulkhead ends, junk spar census, junk rudder); its contact rule caught the rudder floating clear of the transom and was right — not a sixth wrong call. Two ratchet movers classified from full-size diffs and accepted (ship-junk 20.5% is the rebuild; ship-dhow 0.49% is the junk's fan poking into her neighbour's frame edge). Two new baselines committed, ship-treasure and aboard-treasure, both re-capturing at 0.000%. Live stamp verified 1786119344 → 1786123059. I also confirmed the previous unlogged round (card track-distance work, commit 4b59b05) live — it had been pushed without verification or a HANDOFF entry; both are now recorded.

